== Verification

The Verification phase produces the evidence that the product meets its security requirements and withstands attack. It includes four types of security verification and validation testing, from testing what you specified towards testing what an attacker would try.

.*What this phase covers*
[%collapsible]
====
[mermaid]
----
flowchart LR
    SR[Security requirements testing] --> E[Evidence that security works]
    RM[Risk mitigation testing] --> E
    VI[Vulnerability identification testing] --> E
    PT[Penetration testing] --> E
----

* *Security requirements testing* — confirm that each security requirement is implemented as specified. This includes testing that external interfaces validate their input and that the product handles exceptional and fatal error conditions safely.
* *Risk mitigation testing* — attempt to defeat the mitigations of identified threats and risks, to verify that they work as designed rather than only on paper.
* *Vulnerability identification testing* — search the product for weaknesses that nobody specified: verify that hardening matches the attack surface specification, and scan applications, hosts and devices for known vulnerabilities.
* *Penetration testing* — attack the integrated system as an adversary would, to find issues that the other three types cannot reach, such as logic errors and integration flaws.

The controls listed below are the authoritative scope of this phase. Installed extension modules may add further controls here.
====

=== Why it matters

Design and implementation controls reduce the likelihood of vulnerabilities, but verification provides the evidence. The four testing types are deliberately complementary: requirements testing shows that what you specified works, risk mitigation testing shows that your defences hold, vulnerability identification finds what nobody specified, and penetration testing finds what only an attacker would think of. Without them, you cannot demonstrate that security measures actually work — to your own team, to auditors, or to customers.

